          "title": "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "3565dea5-f7ea-5af3-92f1-bc436f89c257",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n\nII. $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n\nIII. $B={A}^{-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/worked-solution": "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $B={A}^{-1}$ and statement III is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\u2217 & \u2217 \\\\ \u2217 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
